{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":5,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.gap_le_tail_of_summing_closes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.gap_le_tail_of_summing_closes","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/GeometricTailBound.lean#L108-L114","doc":"Contrapositive form, which is how the measurement is actually used: if the\nfull sum DOES reach the target, the gap was at most the tail. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.summing_cannot_close","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.summing_cannot_close","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/GeometricTailBound.lean#L83-L106","doc":"**Summing every remaining term cannot reach a target that the tail\nundershoots.**\n\nIf the outstanding gap `C - (1+x)` is `K` times the entire tail with `K > 1`,\nthen even the full geometric sum `(1-x)⁻¹` falls short of `C`.\n\nThis is the honest content of \"we should use all the terms\": one may, and it is\nstill not enough. At `x = ℏ_q³` the measured `K` is `323`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_le","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/GeometricTailBound.lean#L74-L81","doc":"A crude but useful bound: for `0 ≤ x ≤ 1/2` the entire tail is at most\n`2x²`. So \"all the terms\" is second order in a quantity already small. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_pos","kind":"theorem","line":66,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/GeometricTailBound.lean#L66-L72","doc":"The tail is positive when `0 < x < 1`, so summing always moves in the same\ndirection — it just cannot move far. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.GeometricTailBound.tail_eq","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/GeometricTailBound.lean#L58-L64","doc":"**The whole tail of a geometric series past its second term.**\n`(1-x)⁻¹ - (1+x) = x²/(1-x)`, for any `x ≠ 1`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}